Below is a MATLAB program that converts an ultrasound signal from:

\[
\text{Voltage vs Time}
\]

to

\[
\text{Magnitude(dB) vs Frequency(MHz)}
\]

using the Fast Fourier Transform FFT.

---

## MATLAB Program

```matlab
clc;
clear;
close all;

%% Load or define ultrasound signal
% Example: If your data is stored in a text or CSV file
% The file should contain two columns:
% Column 1 = time in seconds
% Column 2 = voltage in volts

data = readmatrix('ultrasound_signal.csv');

t = data(:,1);      % Time vector in seconds
v = data(:,2);      % Voltage signal in volts

%% Sampling information
dt = t(2) - t(1);       % Time step
Fs = 1/dt;              % Sampling frequency in Hz
N = length(v);          % Number of samples

%% Remove DC component
v = v - mean(v);

%% Apply window function to reduce spectral leakage
window = hanning(N);
v_windowed = v .* window;

%% Perform FFT
V_fft = fft(v_windowed);

%% Compute single-sided spectrum
V_mag = abs(V_fft/N);           % Magnitude normalization
V_mag = V_mag(1:N/2+1);         % Single-sided spectrum
V_mag(2:end-1) = 2*V_mag(2:end-1);

%% Frequency axis
f = Fs*(0:(N/2))/N;             % Frequency in Hz
f_MHz = f/1e6;                  % Convert Hz to MHz

%% Convert magnitude to dB
% Reference value is 1 V
Magnitude_dB = 20*log10(V_mag);

% Avoid -Inf values if magnitude is zero
Magnitude_dB(isinf(Magnitude_dB)) = -300;

%% Plot time-domain signal
figure;
plot(t*1e6, v, 'b');
grid on;
xlabel('Time (\mus)');
ylabel('Voltage (V)');
title('Ultrasound Signal in Time Domain');

%% Plot frequency-domain signal
figure;
plot(f_MHz, Magnitude_dB, 'r', 'LineWidth', 1.5);
grid on;
xlabel('Frequency (MHz)');
ylabel('Magnitude (dB)');
title('Frequency Spectrum of Ultrasound Signal');

%% Optional: limit frequency range for ultrasound display
xlim([0 max(f_MHz)]);
```

---

## If You Already Have Time and Voltage Vectors

If your signal is already loaded in MATLAB as `t` and `v`, use this simplified version:

```matlab
dt = t(2) - t(1);
Fs = 1/dt;
N = length(v);

v = v - mean(v);

window = hanning(N);
v_windowed = v .* window;

V_fft = fft(v_windowed);

V_mag = abs(V_fft/N);
V_mag = V_mag(1:N/2+1);
V_mag(2:end-1) = 2*V_mag(2:end-1);

f = Fs*(0:(N/2))/N;
f_MHz = f/1e6;

Magnitude_dB = 20*log10(V_mag);
Magnitude_dB(isinf(Magnitude_dB)) = -300;

figure;
plot(f_MHz, Magnitude_dB);
grid on;
xlabel('Frequency (MHz)');
ylabel('Magnitude (dB)');
title('Magnitude Spectrum of Ultrasound Signal');
```

---

## Notes

- The input signal must be sampled uniformly in time.
- Time should be in seconds.
- Voltage should be in volts.
- Frequency is converted from Hz to MHz using:

```matlab
f_MHz = f / 1e6;
```

- Magnitude in dB is calculated using:

```matlab
Magnitude_dB = 20*log10(V_mag);
```

- If you want the spectrum relative to the maximum magnitude, use:

```matlab
Magnitude_dB = 20*log10(V_mag / max(V_mag));
```

This will make the largest peak equal to `0 dB`.

